hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor today we're going to be  talking about the end old test the indle  test is an example of a biochemical test  that helps us to distinguish between  different kinds of bacteria based on  their biochemical or metabolic  characteristics and the indle test in  particular is one that allows us to  identify bacteria as being either Endel  positive or negative based on their  ability to convert tryptophan which is  an amino acid one of the building blocks  of proteins so to convert tryptophan  into a compound known as Endel using a  chain of enzymes that are collectively  known as a tryptophan in the tryptophan  ace and so this probably makes sense  remember that when we talk about enzymes  we're looking at words ending in ace a  tryptophan ace would be an enzyme or in  this case a group of enzymes that break  down tryptophan which is shown here if  you're used to looking at amino acid  structures there is your alpha carbon  right there with its hydrogen its  carboxyl group and its amino group and  this would be its R group right there  it makes tryptophan so this right here  is tryptophan and it is reacted with  water so it is a kind of hydrolysis  reaction along with those tryptophan ice  enzymes to break tryptophan down to get  three different products so they are in  dole which is this one right here and  pyruvate and an ammonium ion so this is  a kind of deamination reaction  it's called deamination because it's  severing this amino group from the  carbon and it's bound to that's what  gives us the ammonium ions that's why  it's a deamination reaction and you  might be asking yourself well why why  would bacteria even have this chain  events  this trip Tiffany's capability if  tryptophan is an amino acid it's a  building block of proteins well why do  we want to break it down well it turns  out that these products can have a few  different uses in a cell so for example  in dole which is this one right here it  is an intracellular signal molecule so  it is a molecule that can be produced by  a cell and then excreted in order to  allow that cell to talk to other  surrounding cells it helps regulate  activities like biofilm formation and  also certain theory Lentz capabilities  and so that's why a bacterial cell might  need to produce indole by breaking down  tryptophan hmmm pyruvate one of the  other products here you may have heard  of pyruvate before as the end product of  another process called glycolysis so it  turns out that making pyruvate this way  means that it can feed directly into the  Krebs cycle also known as the citric  acid cycle the TCA cycle the  tricarboxylic acid cycle these are all  different names for the same cycle and  that are part of the aerobic respiration  pathway so glycolysis and the Krebs  cycle and the electron transport chain  these things work together to generate  ATP so that's a another way that the  cell if it was needing some ATP might  break down some tryptophan to get the  pyruvate to send it into the Krebs cycle  to help generate that ATP so these both  of these products have some some  necessary effects that sometimes a sound  means so now we are kind of hijacking  this ability to convert tryptophan and  indel as a way in the indole test to  distinguish between different kinds of  bacteria so let's talk about the  procedure so first of all you have a  test organism some bacterial culture  that's been cultured from a patient for  example and in order to identify it you  want to determine  it has this ability to convert  tryptophan to endl or not so you would  knock you laid some sterile media with  this test organism and the sterile media  there's a few different kinds that are  possible one is sim media another's  nutrient peptone really any media that  has enough tryptophan in it to be a  starting reagent for any tryptophan ace  that's present then that maybe it would  be good enough for the annual test but  these are certainly the more common ones  the new incubate at 37 degrees this  human body temperature for 24 to 48  hours and this is to give the test  organism time to convert tryptophan to  Endel if in fact it's able to do so if  it has those tryptophan eeks enzymes  then after 24 to 48 hours you add half a  milliliter Kovacs reagent and that's  like a few drops so it's not not a lot  and then you observe for the presence or  absence of a cherry red color at the  surface so if you've got two tubes and  this one was inoculated with an indole  positive bacterial culture then this  right here the surface solution will get  a bright cherry red color that's because  the chemicals are in this Kovacs reagent  can react with indole if it's present to  create a dino Nazz the rosen dual dye  and that has a cherry red a color  however if there is no indole present  because there was no trip tiffany's to  break down the tryptophan that was in  the media then you end up just kind of  having a yellow color here I don't have  a yellow marker so I'm using orange  today but this would be a sort of a  bright yellow instead of a bright red  and so some examples here of into a  positive bacteria include ecoli era  Mona's hydrophilic and terracotta  cyclist species that would be indle  negative meaning that they don't have  today's enzyme so they cannot convert  tryptophan to indle those would be  species of Salmonella and Klebsiella 2  as an example and there is also a  another kind of form of the indole test  that's called a rapid spot test it's not  as sensitive as this to test and so it's  used less frequently but you might need  to know that that spot test exists if  you are interested in learning more  about enzymes you can see my video on  introduction to enzymes I also have a  video about aerobic respiration where I  talk about the krebs cycle a little bit  if you're interested in that and then I  have a playlist with lots of videos  about many other of these kind of  biochemical identification tests things  like the citrate test the lecithinase  test the DNA's test the indole test and  so if you're interested in learning more  about those and please check out that  playlist and thanks for watching biology  professor today
 

